<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajib al-Kafuta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1611</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ajib the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajib al-Kafuta was the most famous chief of the Abdallab, the Arab rulers of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> who governed as viceroys of the Funj Sultanate. He sought greater independence from the Funj, extending his influence over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the pilgrimage routes, but was defeated and killed by them around 1611, after which the Abdallab remained subordinate.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1611–1612 Sultan Adlan I defeated and killed the Abdallab ruler <b>Ajib</b>, who had rebelled, at Karkoj, reasserting Sennar’s supremacy over its northern vassals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ajib al-Kafuta</b>, the most famous chief, sought greater independence and was killed by the Funj around 1611; afterwards the sheikhs remained subordinate but influential.</blockquote><a href="/state/abdallab-sheikhdom">Abdallab Sheikhdom</a></li></ul></section>
